The machinery under `ca::unicode`.

- `tables.hpp`: the character database, generated by `tooling/gen_unicode.py` (`make unicode`)
  from the running Python and the UCD of its Unicode version; never edited by hand. Python's own
  two-stage layout: `block_of[point >> BLOCK_SHIFT]` picks a block of `record_index`, whose entry
  picks a `char_record`. Code points from `TABLE_LIMIT` up share record 0: no class, mapped to
  themselves.
- `char_record.hpp`: one record per group of code points sharing properties: class flags, the
  simple case mappings as deltas (point + delta), and for code points with full mappings the
  index of their `special_case` (up to three code points per mapping).
- `flags_of.hpp`: the class flags of a code point; `ascii_flags` copies the 128 ASCII entries into
  one flat array at compile time, so ASCII costs a single read.
- `decode_at.hpp`, `decode_before.hpp`: decode one code point forwards from a position, or the one
  ending at a position. Decoding backwards splits invalid sequences exactly as decoding forwards
  would, so a text walked from either end yields the same code points.
- `skip_ascii.hpp`: the fast path of UTF-8 algorithms: the first position, 16 bytes at a time, where
  a byte at or above 0x80 may sit. In constant evaluation it returns its argument and the scalar
  loop runs instead.

Regenerating the tables with a newer Python moves the whole library to its Unicode version, and
`tooling/gen_str_test.py` then regenerates the test that checks the string methods against that
Python.
